Ran the adversarial-check step from the scope's own §2.5 early, against
its own §2.0 precondition, by reading the actual vendored ARC-AGI-3-Agents
framework source instead of leaving it as an unverified working
assumption.

Finding: Swarm.main() instantiates every game's agent up front and runs
them CONCURRENTLY on separate threads within one process, then joins all
of them -- not the sequential single-process loop the scope originally
guessed. This confirms one process is sufficient for in-memory storage
(no scratch file needed) but invalidates the "later games benefit from
earlier games' completed lessons" mental model -- there is no roster
ordering under real concurrency, only racy wall-clock-order signal.

Updated: RunLocalMechanicLedger now specified as thread-safe by
construction (hard correctness requirement, not optional); the §2.4
validation harness must simulate real concurrent play, not a sequential
loop, or its result wouldn't represent the actual deployment shape.

Still scope only -- nothing implemented or enabled.

